---
// Figure: request coalescing inside each process.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import Readout from '../shared/Readout.astro';
import './stampedes.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="st-singleflight" title="Single-flight in each process" advance={1000} legend={['client', 'hit', 'miss', 'write']}
  caption="The same model as Figure 1, at 15 requests a second. With single-flight, a process that is already loading the key makes later requests for it wait for that load; the waiting requests are drawn as filled slots beside the process. Processes do not know about each other.">
  <Choice slot="params" name="mode" label="When a process misses" value="singleflight"
    options={[{ value: 'none', label: 'every request queries' }, { value: 'singleflight', label: 'single-flight' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="stats">
    <Readout name="queries" label="Database queries, latest expiry" kind="database" />
    <Readout name="waiting" label="Requests waiting now" kind="client" />
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>With every request querying, each expiry sends about 15 queries to the database, as in Figure 1. With single-flight, the first request in each process to miss sends a query and the others in the same process wait for its result, so each expiry sends at most four queries, one per process, usually exactly four. The waiting requests are answered when their process's query returns, about one second after the expiry, the same time a miss took before.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { mountHotKey } from './hotkey-scene.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="st-singleflight"]').forEach(root => mountHotKey(root));
</script>
